import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Generic Pattern Match","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Generic Pattern Match","type":"adr","date":"2026-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"Accepted\\n\\nPays the last remaining ADR-0058 debt: pattern matching (`try_pattern`\\nin `match`/`handle`). Follows the direction established by\\n[ADR-0058 — Generic Data-Driven Engines](./0058-generic-data-driven-engines.md)\\n§\\"Existing hardcoded logic is debt\\", and the template of\\n[ADR-0059 — Generic Object Key Evaluation](./0059-generic-object-key-evaluation.md)\\nand [ADR-0060 — Generic Call Dispatch](./0060-generic-call-dispatch.md)."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0061-generic-pattern-match.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0061-generic-pattern-match.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0061-generic-pattern-match.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0061-generic-pattern-match" tabindex="-1">ADR-0061: Generic Pattern Match <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0061-generic-pattern-match" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0061: Generic Pattern Match”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0058 established that eval engines should be fixed interpreters of <code>Language.eval</code> data. ADR-0059 migrated object-key resolution to a desugar rule. ADR-0060 migrated <code>call</code> dispatch to a scheme description. The remaining ADR-0058 debt is <strong>pattern matching</strong> — the <code>try_pattern</code> function and its helpers (<code>try_array_pattern</code>, <code>try_object_pattern</code>, <code>rest_binder_name</code>) are duplicated across all three engines with identical logic:</p><ul><li><strong>Tree-walk</strong>: <code>crates/plugin-engine-exec-tree-walk/src/lib.rs</code></li><li><strong>Bytecode</strong>: <code>crates/plugin-engine-exec-bytecode/src/vm.rs</code></li><li><strong>Cranelift</strong>: <code>crates/plugin-engine-exec-cranelift/src/lib.rs</code></li></ul><p>Each engine&#39;s <code>match</code>/<code>handle</code> prim calls <code>try_pattern</code>, which interprets a pattern AST node structurally against a scrutinee value:</p><ol><li><strong>Wildcard</strong> — <code>var</code> node named <code>_</code> or <code>_*</code> → always matches.</li><li><strong>Binder</strong> — <code>var</code> node with another name → binds name to scrutinee, always matches.</li><li><strong>Literal</strong> — any other node → evaluate and compare by <code>==</code>.</li><li><strong>Array destructuring</strong> — <code>array</code> node with sub-pattern children + optional <code>rest</code> node → positional match, <code>...rest</code> captures tail.</li><li><strong>Object destructuring</strong> — <code>object</code> node with <code>entry</code> children + optional <code>rest</code> node → key match, <code>...rest</code> captures unmatched keys.</li></ol><p>The pattern forms are hardcoded: adding a new pattern form (e.g. <code>or</code> patterns, <code>is</code> type tests, range patterns) requires editing all three engines in lockstep — exactly the violation ADR-0058 names.</p><h3 id="why-pattern-matching-is-harder-than-call-or-object" tabindex="-1">Why pattern matching is harder than <code>call</code> or <code>object</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-pattern-matching-is-harder-than-call-or-object" aria-label="Permalink to “Why pattern matching is harder than call or object”">​</a></h3><p>ADR-0060 migrated <code>call</code> by expressing its two-way callee dispatch as a <code>Choice</code> in <code>eval.yaml</code>. That worked because <code>call</code>&#39;s dispatch is <strong>flat</strong>: one value, one type test, two branches. The <code>Choice</code> combinator is a natural fit.</p><p>Pattern matching is <strong>recursive with stateful bindings</strong>:</p><ul><li>An <code>array</code> pattern contains sub-patterns, each of which may itself be an <code>array</code> or <code>object</code> pattern, recursively. Each sub-pattern may bind variables into the environment.</li><li>The number of arms is variable (one per <code>match</code> clause), and each arm must be tried in order until one matches — a sequential search, not a two-way <code>Choice</code>.</li><li>A failed arm must discard its bindings (scope pop), while a successful arm&#39;s bindings persist into the body evaluation (scope kept).</li><li><code>...rest</code> captures are computed from the <em>remaining</em> elements/keys after positional/entry matching, requiring access to the scrutinee&#39;s structure mid-recursion.</li></ul><p>The kernel combinators (<code>Arr</code>, <code>Seq</code>, <code>First</code>, <code>Choice</code>, <code>Loop</code>, <code>App</code>) can in principle express this — <code>Loop</code> for the arm-by-arm search, <code>Choice</code> for match/no-match, <code>First</code> for pair threading of bindings — but the resulting scheme would be deeply nested, require new native ops for pattern-node-kind dispatch and binding, and be harder to read than the Rust it replaces. The cost/benefit ratio is unfavorable today: the pattern forms are stable, and the duplication is the immediate problem.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><h3 id="de-duplicate-extract-try-pattern-into-a-shared-module" tabindex="-1">De-duplicate: extract <code>try_pattern</code> into a shared module <a class="header-anchor" href="#de-duplicate-extract-try-pattern-into-a-shared-module" aria-label="Permalink to “De-duplicate: extract try_pattern into a shared module”">​</a></h3><p>Extract <code>try_pattern</code>, <code>try_array_pattern</code>, <code>try_object_pattern</code>, and <code>rest_binder_name</code> into a shared module in <code>handl-plugin-engine-exec</code> (the abstraction crate all three engines already depend on). The shared module exposes a <code>PatternMatchCtx</code> trait that abstracts the two engine-specific operations pattern matching needs:</p><ul><li><strong><code>define_binding</code></strong> — bind a name to a value in the current scope (for binder and rest patterns). Each engine&#39;s <code>ScopeStack</code> implements this.</li><li><strong><code>eval_pattern_node</code></strong> — evaluate a pattern AST node as a runtime value (for literal patterns). Each engine&#39;s eval function implements this.</li></ul><p>The shared <code>try_pattern</code> function takes <code>&amp;Ast</code>, <code>AstRef</code> (the pattern node), <code>&amp;Value</code> (the scrutinee), and <code>&amp;mut impl PatternMatchCtx</code>. All pattern-node-kind dispatch (wildcard, binder, array, object, literal) lives in this one function. The three engines&#39; <code>prim_match</code>/<code>prim_handle</code> call the shared <code>try_pattern</code> instead of their own copies.</p><p>This eliminates the 3x duplication: adding a new pattern form is now a one-place change in the shared module, not three engine edits.</p><h3 id="what-stays-in-rust-for-now" tabindex="-1">What stays in Rust (for now) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-stays-in-rust-for-now" aria-label="Permalink to “What stays in Rust (for now)”">​</a></h3><p>The <code>prim_match</code> and <code>prim_handle</code> functions stay in each engine. They contain the arm-iteration loop (evaluate scrutinee, iterate arms, push/pop scope, call <code>try_pattern</code>, evaluate body on match). This logic is thin — it&#39;s the <code>Choice</code>/<code>Loop</code> structure that the kernel combinators would express — and it delegates all pattern interpretation to the shared module.</p><p>The <code>match</code> vs <code>handle</code> semantic difference (match errors on no arm, handle returns the body value) stays in the engine-level prim, where it belongs: it&#39;s a control-flow decision, not a pattern-form decision.</p><h3 id="path-to-full-data-driven-future-work" tabindex="-1">Path to full data-driven (future work) <a class="header-anchor" href="#path-to-full-data-driven-future-work" aria-label="Permalink to “Path to full data-driven (future work)”">​</a></h3><p>A future ADR can migrate pattern matching fully into <code>eval.yaml</code> when the kernel gains the combinators needed to express it cleanly. The likely shape:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Desugar patterns into match-op AST nodes.</strong> Each pattern form becomes a node kind with a scheme: <code>pattern_wildcard</code> (always matches), <code>pattern_binder</code> (binds name), <code>pattern_literal</code> (eval + compare), <code>pattern_array</code> (positional + rest), <code>pattern_object</code> (keyed + rest). The desugar pass rewrites <code>arm</code> children from arbitrary AST nodes into these canonical pattern nodes.</p></li><li><p><strong>Express <code>match</code>/<code>handle</code> as <code>Loop</code> + <code>Choice</code>.</strong> The arm-by-arm search becomes a <code>Loop</code> whose body tries one arm: <code>Seq</code> of <code>try_pattern_arm</code> (a native op that calls the shared <code>try_pattern</code> and returns an <code>Either</code> — <code>Left(bindings)</code> on match, <code>Right(())</code> on no match) and a <code>Choice</code> that either breaks the loop (match → eval body) or continues (no match → next arm).</p></li><li><p><strong>The shared <code>try_pattern</code> becomes the <code>try_pattern_arm</code> native op.</strong> The pattern-form dispatch stays in Rust (it&#39;s a fixed interpreter of pattern-node data), but the arm-iteration and scope management move to the scheme level.</p></li></ol><p>This path is deferred because the kernel combinators needed for variable-length arm iteration (<code>Loop</code> with early exit via <code>Choice</code>) are not yet exercised in this pattern, and the desugar infrastructure for patterns would need new <code>AstShapeDef</code> entries for each pattern form. The de-duplication in this ADR is the prerequisite: it consolidates the pattern-form dispatch into one place, so the future migration changes one module, not three engines.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>One implementation, not three.</strong> <code>try_pattern</code> and its helpers live in <code>handl-plugin-engine-exec::pattern_match</code>. Adding a new pattern form (or-patters, type tests, ranges) is a one-place change.</p></li><li><p><strong>Engines converge further.</strong> Each engine&#39;s <code>prim_match</code>/<code>prim_handle</code> is reduced to the arm-iteration loop + body evaluation. All pattern interpretation is shared.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>PatternMatchCtx</code> trait is minimal.</strong> Two methods (<code>define_binding</code>, <code>eval_pattern_node</code>) — the smallest interface that captures the engine-specific operations pattern matching needs. No new kernel combinators, no new <code>ArrSource</code> variants.</p></li><li><p><strong>Tests are preserved.</strong> The existing pattern matching tests (<code>match_wildcard_pattern</code>, <code>match_binder_pattern</code>, <code>match_literal_pattern</code>, <code>match_array_destructuring</code>, <code>match_array_rest</code>, <code>match_object_destructuring</code>, <code>match_object_rest</code>, <code>handle_dispatches_to_matching_clause</code>, <code>handle_no_clause_returns_body</code>) and integration tests in <code>fp_integration.rs</code> become the conformance baseline. The behavior they assert is preserved exactly; only the location of the implementation changes.</p></li><li><p><strong>ADR-0058&#39;s pattern matching debt is paid.</strong> ADR-0058 §&quot;Existing hardcoded logic is debt&quot; named pattern matching as the last deferred item. This ADR pays it by eliminating the 3x duplication and consolidating pattern-form dispatch into one shared module. The full data-driven migration (schemes in <code>eval.yaml</code>) is documented as future work with a clear path.</p></li></ul>`,27)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};